# Nordshrift Network Component Model — NS-NET-0001

Status: implementation model
Revision: 1.0.0
Date: September 2026

## Purpose

This model defines the network components exposed to Nordshrift/Sleela builds.
The network layer is deliberately target-neutral: the same component graph can
be represented in Sleela source, emitted as C-backed execution, or represented
as SecureJDK 28 `.xclass` structural metadata.

## Components

- `Endpoint` — logical communication endpoint
- `NIC` — network interface attached to an endpoint
- `Link` — point-to-point transport path with latency/capacity metadata
- `Packet` — transferable network unit
- `Queue` — bounded packet/event queue
- `Switch` — layer-2 forwarding component
- `Router` — layer-3 forwarding component
- `Fabric` — collection of interconnected links and forwarding nodes
- `Listener` — passive server-side connection endpoint
- `Connector` — active client-side connection endpoint
- `Gateway` — boundary between network domains
- `LoadBalancer` — service-selection and distribution component
- `Service` — named network service endpoint
- `TLS` — transport security policy component
- `DNS` — name-resolution component

## Compile targets

The Nordshrift target vocabulary is extended conceptually to:

- `java` — Java source
- `sleela` — canonical `.sleela` source, executable on the C core
- `c` — C source using the Sleela runtime model
- `xclass` — SecureJDK 28 XML class structure

`.xclass` is structural: it carries class identity, fields, methods, and
metadata rather than executable bytecode. The existing XCI-0001 loader can
ingest the result back into a runnable Sleela program.

## Network execution ABI

The core VM exposes network operations as VM opcodes with VM-local socket
handles: `LISTEN`, `ACCEPT`, `CONNECT`, `SOCKREAD`, `SOCKWRITE`, and `SOCKCLOSE`.
These operations are compatible with the existing Sleela networking built-ins
and the loopback socket smoke test.

## SST guidance

A network-oriented sheet should declare its component set and select a target,
for example:

```sst
#nordshrift 1.0
#sleela 1.0

sheet network-demo:
  version 1.0.0
  author "Sleela Design Council"
  description "Network component compilation test"

source:
  root "src"
  glob "**/*.sleela"

target:
  root "out"
  layout mirror-source
  java-version 21
  target-language sleela

network:
  components [ Endpoint, NIC, Link, Packet, Queue, Switch, Router, Fabric,
               Listener, Connector, Gateway, LoadBalancer, Service, TLS, DNS ]
  transports [ tcp, udp ]
  topology mesh
  address-family dual
  security tls
```

The network block is metadata for the compilation model; executable network
behavior remains expressed by Sleela built-ins and lowered into the core ABI.
